  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "At a band audition, there are $50$ singers. There are $27$ country singers, $23$ pop singers, and $6$ country and pop singers. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a singer is selected at random among the $50$ singers, what is the probability that the singer sings country or pop music (or both)? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let us call $A$ the event \"the singer sings country music\" and $B$ the event \"the singer sings pop music.\"\n\nNote that there are $6$ singers singing both country and pop music. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $6$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing country music is\n\n$$\n27-6=21,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $21$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing pop music is\n\n$$\n23-6=17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of singers who sing country or pop music is\n\n$$\n6+21+17=44,\n$$\n\nand there are $50$ singers in total, so the number of remaining singers is\n\n$$\n50-44=6.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $6$ singers who did not sing country or pop music, we write the number $6$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/0240b83c3de5c8c3f3b1e91d3b9c8ae2899f0b2a18611d7e70b6dbf71f23e25b.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to singers that sing country or pop music (or both). The probability that a randomly selected singer is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$",
